#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:50
msgid "bpf_jit_enable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:52
msgid ""
"This enables the BPF Just in Time (JIT) compiler. BPF is a flexible and "
"efficient infrastructure allowing to execute bytecode at various hook "
"points. It is used in a number of Linux kernel subsystems such as networking "
"(e.g. XDP, tc), tracing (e.g. kprobes, uprobes, tracepoints) and security (e."
"g. seccomp). LLVM has a BPF back end that can compile restricted C into a "
"sequence of BPF instructions. After program load through bpf(2) and passing "
"a verifier in the kernel, a JIT will then translate these BPF proglets into "
"native CPU instructions. There are two flavors of JITs, the newer eBPF JIT "
"currently supported on:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:62
msgid "x86_64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:63
msgid "x86_32"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:64
msgid "arm64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:65
msgid "arm32"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:66
msgid "ppc64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:67
msgid "ppc32"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:68
msgid "sparc64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:69
msgid "mips64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:70
msgid "s390x"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:71
msgid "riscv64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:72
msgid "riscv32"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:73
msgid "loongarch64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:74
msgid "arc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:76
msgid "And the older cBPF JIT supported on the following archs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:78
msgid "mips"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:79
msgid "sparc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:81
msgid ""
"eBPF JITs are a superset of cBPF JITs, meaning the kernel will migrate cBPF "
"instructions into eBPF instructions and then JIT compile them transparently. "
"Older cBPF JITs can only translate tcpdump filters, seccomp rules, etc, but "
"not mentioned eBPF programs loaded through bpf(2)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:87
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:100
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:524
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:543
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:583
msgid "Values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:89
msgid "0 - disable the JIT (default value)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:90
msgid "1 - enable the JIT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:91
msgid "2 - enable the JIT and ask the compiler to emit traces on kernel log."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:94
msgid "bpf_jit_harden"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:96
msgid ""
"This enables hardening for the BPF JIT compiler. Supported are eBPF JIT "
"backends. Enabling hardening trades off performance, but can mitigate JIT "
"spraying."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:102
msgid "0 - disable JIT hardening (default value)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:103
msgid "1 - enable JIT hardening for unprivileged users only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:104
msgid "2 - enable JIT hardening for all users"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:106
msgid ""
"where \"privileged user\" in this context means a process having CAP_BPF or "
"CAP_SYS_ADMIN in the root user name space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:110
msgid "bpf_jit_kallsyms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:112
msgid ""
"When BPF JIT compiler is enabled, then compiled images are unknown addresses "
"to the kernel, meaning they neither show up in traces nor in /proc/kallsyms. "
"This enables export of these addresses, which can be used for debugging/"
"tracing. If bpf_jit_harden is enabled, this feature is disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:118
msgid "Values :"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:120
msgid "0 - disable JIT kallsyms export (default value)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:121
msgid "1 - enable JIT kallsyms export for privileged users only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:124
msgid "bpf_jit_limit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:126
msgid ""
"This enforces a global limit for memory allocations to the BPF JIT compiler "
"in order to reject unprivileged JIT requests once it has been surpassed. "
"bpf_jit_limit contains the value of the global limit in bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:132
msgid "dev_weight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:134
msgid ""
"The maximum number of packets that kernel can handle on a NAPI interrupt, "
"it's a Per-CPU variable. For drivers that support LRO or GRO_HW, a hardware "
"aggregated packet is counted as one packet in this context."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:138
msgid "Default: 64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:141
msgid "dev_weight_rx_bias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:143
msgid ""
"RPS (e.g. RFS, aRFS) processing is competing with the registered NAPI poll "
"function of the driver for the per softirq cycle netdev_budget. This "
"parameter influences the proportion of the configured netdev_budget that is "
"spent on RPS based packet processing during RX softirq cycles. It is further "
"meant for making current dev_weight adaptable for asymmetric CPU needs on RX/"
"TX side of the network stack. (see dev_weight_tx_bias) It is effective on a "
"per CPU basis. Determination is based on dev_weight and is calculated "
"multiplicative (dev_weight * dev_weight_rx_bias)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:151
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:162
msgid "Default: 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:154
msgid "dev_weight_tx_bias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:156
msgid ""
"Scales the maximum number of packets that can be processed during a TX "
"softirq cycle. Effective on a per CPU basis. Allows scaling of current "
"dev_weight for asymmetric net stack processing needs. Be careful to avoid "
"making TX softirq processing a CPU hog."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:160
msgid "Calculation is based on dev_weight (dev_weight * dev_weight_tx_bias)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:165
msgid "default_qdisc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:167
msgid ""
"The default queuing discipline to use for network devices. This allows "
"overriding the default of pfifo_fast with an alternative. Since the default "
"queuing discipline is created without additional parameters so is best "
"suited to queuing disciplines that work well without configuration like "
"stochastic fair queue (sfq), CoDel (codel) or fair queue CoDel (fq_codel). "
"Don't use queuing disciplines like Hierarchical Token Bucket or Deficit "
"Round Robin which require setting up classes and bandwidths. Note that "
"physical multiqueue interfaces still use mq as root qdisc, which in turn "
"uses this default for its leaves. Virtual devices (like e.g. lo or veth) "
"ignore this setting and instead default to noqueue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:178
msgid "Default: pfifo_fast"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:181
msgid "busy_read"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:183
msgid ""
"Low latency busy poll timeout for socket reads. (needs "
"CONFIG_NET_RX_BUSY_POLL) Approximate time in us to busy loop waiting for "
"packets on the device queue. This sets the default value of the SO_BUSY_POLL "
"socket option. Can be set or overridden per socket by setting socket option "
"SO_BUSY_POLL, which is the preferred method of enabling. If you need to "
"enable the feature globally via sysctl, a value of 50 is recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:190
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:205
msgid "Will increase power usage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:192
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:207
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:220
msgid "Default: 0 (off)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:195
msgid "busy_poll"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:196
msgid ""
"Low latency busy poll timeout for poll and select. (needs "
"CONFIG_NET_RX_BUSY_POLL) Approximate time in us to busy loop waiting for "
"events. Recommended value depends on the number of sockets you poll on. For "
"several sockets 50, for several hundreds 100. For more than that you "
"probably want to use epoll. Note that only sockets with SO_BUSY_POLL set "
"will be busy polled, so you want to either selectively set SO_BUSY_POLL on "
"those sockets or set sysctl.net.busy_read globally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:210
msgid "mem_pcpu_rsv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:212
msgid ""
"Per-cpu reserved forward alloc cache size in page units. Default 1MB per CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:215
msgid "bypass_prot_mem"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:217
msgid ""
"Skip charging socket buffers to the global per-protocol memory accounting "
"controlled by net.ipv4.tcp_mem, net.ipv4.udp_mem, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:223
msgid "rmem_default"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:225
msgid "The default setting of the socket receive buffer in bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:228
msgid "rmem_max"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:230
msgid "The maximum receive socket buffer size in bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:232
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:259
msgid "Default: 4194304"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:235
msgid "rps_default_mask"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:237
msgid ""
"The default RPS CPU mask used on newly created network devices. An empty "
"mask means RPS disabled by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:241
msgid "tstamp_allow_data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:242
msgid ""
"Allow processes to receive tx timestamps looped together with the original "
"packet contents. If disabled, transmit timestamp requests from unprivileged "
"processes are dropped unless socket option SOF_TIMESTAMPING_OPT_TSONLY is "
"set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:246
msgid "Default: 1 (on)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:250
msgid "wmem_default"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:252
msgid "The default setting (in bytes) of the socket send buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:255
msgid "wmem_max"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:257
msgid "The maximum send socket buffer size in bytes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:262
msgid "message_burst and message_cost"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:264
msgid ""
"These parameters  are used to limit the warning messages written to the "
"kernel log from  the  networking  code.  They  enforce  a  rate  limit  to  "
"make  a denial-of-service attack  impossible. A higher message_cost factor, "
"results in fewer messages that will be written. Message_burst controls when "
"messages will be dropped.  The  default  settings  limit  warning messages "
"to one every five seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:272
msgid "warnings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:274
msgid "This sysctl is now unused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:276
msgid ""
"This was used to control console messages from the networking stack that "
"occur because of problems on the network like duplicate address or bad "
"checksums."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:280
msgid ""
"These messages are now emitted at KERN_DEBUG and can generally be enabled "
"and controlled by the dynamic_debug facility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:284
msgid "netdev_budget"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:286
msgid ""
"Maximum number of packets taken from all interfaces in one polling cycle "
"(NAPI poll). In one polling cycle interfaces which are registered to polling "
"are probed in a round-robin manner. Also, a polling cycle may not exceed "
"netdev_budget_usecs microseconds, even if netdev_budget has not been "
"exhausted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:293
msgid "netdev_budget_usecs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:295
msgid ""
"Maximum number of microseconds in one NAPI polling cycle. Polling will exit "
"when either netdev_budget_usecs have elapsed during the poll cycle or the "
"number of packets processed reaches netdev_budget."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:300
msgid "netdev_max_backlog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:302
msgid ""
"Maximum number of packets, queued on the INPUT side, when the interface "
"receives packets faster than kernel can process them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:306
msgid "qdisc_max_burst"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:308
msgid ""
"Maximum number of packets that can be temporarily stored before reaching "
"qdisc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:311
msgid "Default: 1000"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:314
msgid "netdev_rss_key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:316
msgid ""
"RSS (Receive Side Scaling) enabled drivers use a host key that is randomly "
"generated. Some user space might need to gather its content even if drivers "
"do not provide ethtool -x support yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:326
msgid ""
"File contains all nul bytes if no driver ever called netdev_rss_key_fill() "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:329
msgid "Note:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:330
msgid ""
"/proc/sys/net/core/netdev_rss_key contains 256 bytes of key, but many "
"drivers only use 40 or 52 bytes of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:342
msgid "netdev_tstamp_prequeue"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:344
msgid ""
"If set to 0, RX packet timestamps can be sampled after RPS processing, when "
"the target CPU processes packets. It might give some delay on timestamps, "
"but permit to distribute the load on several cpus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:348
msgid ""
"If set to 1 (default), timestamps are sampled as soon as possible, before "
"queueing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:352
msgid "netdev_unregister_timeout_secs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:354
msgid ""
"Unregister network device timeout in seconds. This option controls the "
"timeout (in seconds) used to issue a warning while waiting for a network "
"device refcount to drop to 0 during device unregistration. A lower value may "
"be useful during bisection to detect a leaked reference faster. A larger "
"value may be useful to prevent false warnings on slow/loaded systems. "
"Default value is 10, minimum 1, maximum 3600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:363
msgid "skb_defer_max"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:365
msgid ""
"Max size (in skbs) of the per-cpu list of skbs being freed by the cpu which "
"allocated them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:368
msgid "Default: 128"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:371
msgid "optmem_max"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:373
msgid ""
"Maximum ancillary buffer size allowed per socket. Ancillary data is a "
"sequence of struct cmsghdr structures with appended data. TCP tx zerocopy "
"also uses optmem_max as a limit for its internal structures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:377
msgid "Default : 128 KB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:380
msgid "fb_tunnels_only_for_init_net"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:382
msgid ""
"Controls if fallback tunnels (like tunl0, gre0, gretap0, erspan0, sit0, "
"ip6tnl0, ip6gre0) are automatically created. There are 3 possibilities (a) "
"value = 0; respective fallback tunnels are created when module is loaded in "
"every net namespaces (backward compatible behavior). (b) value = 1; [kcmd "
"value: initns] respective fallback tunnels are created only in init net "
"namespace and every other net namespace will not have them. (c) value = 2; "
"[kcmd value: none] fallback tunnels are not created when a module is loaded "
"in any of the net namespace. Setting value to \"2\" is pointless after boot "
"if these modules are built-in, so there is a kernel command-line option that "
"can change this default. Please refer to Documentation/admin-guide/kernel-"
"parameters.txt for additional details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:395
msgid ""
"Not creating fallback tunnels gives control to userspace to create whatever "
"is needed only and avoid creating devices which are redundant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:398
#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:414
msgid "Default : 0  (for compatibility reasons)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:401
msgid "devconf_inherit_init_net"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:403
msgid ""
"Controls if a new network namespace should inherit all current settings "
"under /proc/sys/net/{ipv4,ipv6}/conf/{all,default}/. By default, we keep the "
"current behavior: for IPv4 we inherit all current settings from init_net and "
"for IPv6 we reset all settings to default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:408
msgid ""
"If set to 1, both IPv4 and IPv6 settings are forced to inherit from current "
"ones in init_net. If set to 2, both IPv4 and IPv6 settings are forced to "
"reset to their default values. If set to 3, both IPv4 and IPv6 settings are "
"forced to inherit from current ones in the netns where this new netns has "
"been created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:417
msgid "txrehash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:419
msgid ""
"Controls default hash rethink behaviour on socket when SO_TXREHASH option is "
"set to SOCK_TXREHASH_DEFAULT (i. e. not overridden by setsockopt)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:422
msgid ""
"If set to 1 (default), hash rethink is performed on listening socket. If set "
"to 0, hash rethink is not performed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:426
msgid "txq_reselection_ms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:428
msgid ""
"Controls how often (in ms) a busy connected flow can select another tx queue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:430
msgid ""
"A resection is desirable when/if user thread has migrated and XPS would "
"select a different queue. Same can occur without XPS if the flow hash has "
"changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:434
msgid ""
"But switching txq can introduce reorders, especially if the old queue is "
"under high pressure. Modern TCP stacks deal well with reorders if they "
"happen not too often."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:438
msgid "To disable this feature, set the value to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:440
msgid "Default : 1000"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:443
msgid "gro_normal_batch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:445
msgid ""
"Maximum number of the segments to batch up on output of GRO. When a packet "
"exits GRO, either as a coalesced superframe or as an original packet which "
"GRO has decided not to coalesce, it is placed on a per-NAPI list. This list "
"is then passed to the stack when the number of segments reaches the "
"gro_normal_batch limit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:452
msgid "high_order_alloc_disable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:454
msgid ""
"By default the allocator for page frags tries to use high order pages "
"(order-3 on x86). While the default behavior gives good results in most "
"cases, some users might have hit a contention in page allocations/freeing. "
"This was especially true on older kernels (< 5.14) when high-order pages "
"were not stored on per-cpu lists. This allows to opt-in for order-0 "
"allocation instead but is now mostly of historical importance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:461
msgid "Default: 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:464
msgid "2. /proc/sys/net/unix - Parameters for Unix domain sockets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:466
msgid ""
"There is only one file in this directory. unix_dgram_qlen limits the max "
"number of datagrams queued in Unix domain socket's buffer. It will not take "
"effect unless PF_UNIX flag is specified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:472
msgid "3. /proc/sys/net/ipv4 - IPV4 settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:473
msgid ""
"Please see: Documentation/networking/ip-sysctl.rst and Documentation/admin-"
"guide/sysctl/net.rst for descriptions of these entries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:478
msgid "4. TIPC"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:481
msgid "tipc_rmem"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:483
msgid ""
"The TIPC protocol now has a tunable for the receive memory, similar to the "
"tcp_rmem - i.e. a vector of 3 INTEGERs: (min, default, max)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:492
msgid ""
"The max value is set to CONN_OVERLOAD_LIMIT, and the default and min values "
"are scaled (shifted) versions of that same value.  Note that the min value "
"is not at this point in time used in any meaningful way, but the triplet is "
"preserved in order to be consistent with things like tcp_rmem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:498
msgid "named_timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:500
msgid ""
"TIPC name table updates are distributed asynchronously in a cluster, without "
"any form of transaction handling. This means that different race scenarios "
"are possible. One such is that a name withdrawal sent out by one node and "
"received by another node may arrive after a second, overlapping name "
"publication already has been accepted from a third node, although the "
"conflicting updates originally may have been issued in the correct "
"sequential order. If named_timeout is nonzero, failed topology updates will "
"be placed on a defer queue until another event arrives that clears the "
"error, or until the timeout expires. Value is in milliseconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:511
msgid "6. /proc/sys/net/vsock - VSOCK sockets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:513
msgid ""
"VSOCK sockets (AF_VSOCK) provide communication between virtual machines and "
"their hosts. The behavior of VSOCK sockets in a network namespace is "
"determined by the namespace's mode (``global`` or ``local``), which controls "
"how CIDs (Context IDs) are allocated and how sockets interact across "
"namespaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:519
msgid "ns_mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:521
msgid ""
"Read-only. Reports the current namespace's mode, set at namespace creation "
"and immutable thereafter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:526
msgid ""
"``global`` - the namespace shares system-wide CID allocation and its sockets "
"can reach any VM or socket in any global namespace. Sockets in this "
"namespace cannot reach sockets in local namespaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:530
msgid ""
"``local`` - the namespace has private CID allocation and its sockets can "
"only connect to VMs or sockets within the same namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:534
msgid "The init_net mode is always ``global``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:537
msgid "child_ns_mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:539
msgid ""
"Controls what mode newly created child namespaces will inherit. At namespace "
"creation, ``ns_mode`` is inherited from the parent's ``child_ns_mode``. The "
"initial value matches the namespace's own ``ns_mode``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:545
msgid ""
"``global`` - child namespaces will share system-wide CID allocation and "
"their sockets will be able to reach any VM or socket in any global namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:548
msgid ""
"``local`` - child namespaces will have private CID allocation and their "
"sockets will only be able to connect within their own namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:552
msgid ""
"The first write to ``child_ns_mode`` locks its value. Subsequent writes of "
"the same value succeed, but writing a different value returns ``-EBUSY``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:555
msgid ""
"Changing ``child_ns_mode`` only affects namespaces created after the change; "
"it does not modify the current namespace or any existing children."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:558
msgid ""
"A namespace with ``ns_mode`` set to ``local`` cannot change "
"``child_ns_mode`` to ``global`` (returns ``-EPERM``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:562
msgid "g2h_fallback"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:564
msgid ""
"Controls whether connections to CIDs not owned by the host-to-guest (H2G) "
"transport automatically fall back to the guest-to-host (G2H) transport."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:567
msgid ""
"When enabled, if a connect targets a CID that the H2G transport (e.g. vhost-"
"vsock) does not serve, or if no H2G transport is loaded at all, the "
"connection is routed via the G2H transport (e.g. virtio-vsock) instead. This "
"allows a host running both nested VMs (via vhost-vsock) and sibling VMs "
"reachable through the hypervisor (e.g. Nitro Enclaves) to address both using "
"a single CID space, without requiring applications to set "
"``VMADDR_FLAG_TO_HOST``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:575
msgid ""
"When the fallback is taken, ``VMADDR_FLAG_TO_HOST`` is automatically set on "
"the remote address so that userspace can determine the path via "
"``getpeername()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:579
msgid ""
"Note: With this sysctl enabled, user space that attempts to talk to a guest "
"CID which is not implemented by the H2G transport will create host vsock "
"traffic. Environments that rely on H2G-only isolation should set it to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:585
msgid ""
"0 - Connections to CIDs <= 2 or with VMADDR_FLAG_TO_HOST use G2H; all others "
"use H2G (or fail with ENODEV if H2G is not loaded)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst:587
msgid "1 - Connections to CIDs not owned by H2G fall back to G2H. (default)"
msgstr ""
